window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][56] = "<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd begin by understanding each goal separately and in depth. Suppose the head of sales wants a longer free trial to raise conversion, and the head of finance wants a shorter one to protect cash. I would ask each what they're optimizing, what number they would watch, and what would change their mind. Usually the disagreement is over different time horizons or measures, not over facts.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd look for a shared goal: 'We both want profitable growth.' I'd turn the question into options with numbers: 7 days, 14 days, or 14 days with a day-7 reminder; and agree with both the criteria beforehand, for example, 'payback within 6 months and conversion up at least a point.' If a cheap test can settle it, I'd run it. If not, I'd write a one-page decision document with the options, my recommendation and the evidence that would change it, and take it to the person who owns the decision above both of them, having shown each stakeholder the document first so neither is surprised. I'd tell them both what was decided and why, and credit what each contributed. I'd avoid picking the more senior person by default, and I'd avoid a compromise that serves neither goal.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Deep understanding of both sides; criteria before the choice; options; no surprises; fairness.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the common manager won't decide? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what is making it hard (missing information, a political cost) and address that; and if there is no decision, I'd propose a time-boxed trial with the criteria agreed, so that the decision is made by the data after two weeks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if one stakeholder goes around you to the executive? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd speak to them directly and without accusation: 'I heard you took it to [the executive]; I want to make sure you have the full picture; here is the document I'm preparing.' And I'd make sure the executive has the same one-page view.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-07 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You must decide with about 60% of the data you'd like. How do you communicate your recommendation?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd say I'm not sure and ask for more time.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the recommendation with a confidence level.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the uncertainty statement: what we know, assume, confidence as a number, reversibility, what would change the decision and when it will be checked; time-boxes further data; recommends anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd give a clear recommendation, say how sure I am, and say how we'll find out if I'm wrong. For example: 'Based on a two-week test of 8,000 users, conversion rose 2.1% (interval 0.6% to 3.6%). That is the 60% of the data I'd like: we haven't seen a full month or the mobile app. The interval is above zero, but it leaves out novelty and the mobile app, so I'm about 80% confident the effect holds, and I'd expect it to be between 1% and 3%. The change is reversible within a day. So I recommend shipping to 25% of users now and checking again Friday, with a rule: if conversion falls by more than 1%, we roll back. Waiting for the full month costs us about $20,000 in forgone conversions and would reduce my uncertainty only a little, because the interval is already narrowing.'</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd say what I assume (that the second half of the month looks like the first) and what would change my mind (a drop on mobile). I'd put a date on the next look. And I would not hide behind the uncertainty: after stating it, I recommend.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> A recommendation with calibrated confidence; reversibility; a trigger and a check date; the cost of waiting.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How did you get to &quot;80% confident&quot;? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;From the interval: most of it lies above zero, so with a simple model there's a high chance the true effect is positive; I round down because of what we haven't seen. I'd say 'about 80%' to signal it's a judgment, and I'd be happy to hear if someone sees it differently.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the stakes were irreversible? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd want more data, and I'd say what the cost of waiting is, and I'd set a deadline for the decision so that waiting doesn't become a decision by default. I'd also look for a reversible way to learn more first, such as a limited pilot.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-08 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You work with a team about twelve hours away in time. How do you run a project?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd schedule meetings at a time that works for both.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses asynchronous writing, handoffs and limited overlap hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes for asynchronous reading, protects overlap for discussion and decisions, hands off with a written note, shares the inconvenience of timing, makes decisions and ownership explicit, and checks understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd assume we can't rely on live time, and design for that. Everything that needs a decision or has context goes in a document with the answer, the ask and the deadline in the first paragraph, so that the other side can act while I'm asleep. For overlap, say two hours a day, I'd keep it for what needs live discussion: disagreements, complex tradeoffs and relationship. Everything else goes into writing.</p>\n<p>&quot;At the end of my day I'd write a handoff: what's done, what's blocked, what's next and what I need by when. They'd do the same for me. For meetings I'd rotate the awkward time so that one side isn't always stretching, with an agenda and notes with decisions and owners afterward. I'd make ownership explicit, since silence at a distance is easy to misread, and I'd check understanding by asking the other side to restate the decision. And I'd invest in the relationship early, with a call to get to know each other, since trust makes asynchronous work easier.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Async-first writing; protected overlap; handoffs; fairness; explicit ownership.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> A decision is blocking and the other side is asleep. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;If it is reversible and within my authority, decide and document why, and tell them first thing; if not, I'd ask the owner for a delegate who can decide in my hours, or I'd prepare the decision document so that they can approve within minutes when they wake.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep from misreading tone in chat? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'm explicit and a little warmer in writing than feels natural, I say what I want, and I move anything with an edge to a call.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-09 · Intermediate · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How do you make sure a message is understood by engineers, designers and legal?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I send the same message to everyone.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tailors the message to each audience's concerns.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts from shared facts, gives each audience the version that answers their question, avoids jargon, checks understanding by asking for a restatement, and keeps one source of truth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I keep one source of truth, a short document with the facts, the decision and the ask, and give each group the framing that answers their question. Engineers want the scope, the constraint and the date: what exactly are we building, what is out, what are the risks? Designers want the user problem, the evidence and the freedom to solve it: what are we trying to change for whom? Legal wants the exposure, the data involved and the markets, and wants it early. I avoid jargon that one group knows and another doesn't, and I define terms once.</p>\n<p>&quot;I check that the message landed by asking for a restatement ('Can you tell me in your words what we're doing and what you need from me?') or by asking someone outside the team to read the document and say the point. If two groups hear different things, I bring them together with the same one-pager. And I send decisions in writing after a meeting so that memory is not the record.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> One truth, several framings; audience's concerns; checking understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if legal's concerns arrive after the design is done? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd treat it as a lesson about process: bring legal in at the problem stage in the future, not the end. For now I'd ask what is the smallest change that meets the requirement, and present options to design with the real constraint.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you explain a technical constraint to a designer without condescension? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I ask the engineer to explain it in terms of what the user would experience, not how the system works, and I bring examples. I frame it as a design constraint to work within, not as a no.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-10 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You disagree with a data scientist's interpretation of an experiment. How do you resolve it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd trust the data scientist,&quot; or &quot;I'd argue my view.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the method and looks at the data together.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Restates their interpretation, agrees the question and the metric, examines assumptions (sample, duration, novelty, segments), proposes a check that could settle it, and decides who decides and how to proceed in the meantime.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd start by making sure I understand their reading and what it rests on: 'Walk me through how you got to this. What does the interval say, and what would make you change your mind?' I often learn something. Then I'd say my concern concretely: 'I'm wondering whether the lift is novelty, since the first week was +4% and the last was +1%.' We'd agree what question the test answers and which metric is primary, because many disagreements are about different questions.</p>\n<p>&quot;Then I'd look for what would settle it: reading the effect by week, checking segments, extending the test or running a holdout. We'd agree in advance what each result would mean for the decision. If we still disagree, we'd write both readings with the uncertainty and take the decision to the owner, with a recommendation about the next step that is reversible. I'd credit the data scientist's contribution, and if the data shows I was wrong, I'd say so. My aim is the right decision and a working relationship, since I'll need their judgment again.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Understanding the method; a specific concern; agreed criteria; humility; a path to a decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the data scientist is much more senior? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Seniority doesn't change the data. I'd bring the specific concern with evidence and ask for their view on it; senior people usually welcome a precise challenge. If they hold their view, I'd ask what check would convince them, and we'd run it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you realize you misunderstood the analysis? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I say so promptly and thank them. It's cheaper to be wrong in a conversation than in a launch, and it makes the next disagreement easier.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c32-11\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C32-11 · Hard · behavioral · BE · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Write the message to executives that a key launch will slip by three weeks because another team's dependency is late.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A long message that apologizes, blames the other team and leaves the ask unclear.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the new date, the cause, the impact and the plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Facts without blame, a calibrated date with a range, options and a recommendation, what is being done, what is needed and by when, and an offer to discuss; written so that a reader can forward it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; I describe a writing style and make no claim about any real company's practice.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Subject: Launch of the new payouts flow moves from 12 November to 3 December; decision needed by Thursday</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Summary.</strong> The launch will slip three weeks, to 3 December. I'm about 70% confident of that date and would plan on 10 December as the date we can stand behind.</p>\n<p><strong>Cause.</strong> The identity-verification service we depend on will be ready on 24 November, not 3 November. The date was set by the team that owns it; we found out on 28 October when their testing found a defect.</p>\n<p><strong>Impact.</strong> We miss the start of the holiday campaign; about 15% of planned volume (about $400,000) moves into December.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> (A) Ship everything on 3 December. (B) Ship on 12 November without identity verification for returning users (about 80% of volume), adding new users on 3 December. Risk: a split campaign message and a lower verification rate in the interim, which we would cap with limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> B, if marketing confirms it can run a partial campaign.</p>\n<p><strong>What I need.</strong> Your decision on A or B by Thursday, so marketing and engineering can plan.</p>\n<p><strong>What we're doing.</strong> Daily check-ins with the owning team; we've agreed milestones on 12 and 19 November; I'll update you every Tuesday and immediately if the date moves.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>Why it works.</em> The subject line has the news and the ask. It states facts about the cause without blame (&quot;the date was set by the team that owns it&quot;), gives a range, and offers options with a recommendation and a deadline. It can be forwarded without edits.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Do you name the other team? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Yes, factually, since leaders need to know whom to talk to, and without judgment. I'd have already told that team's lead what I'm sending, so it is not a surprise, and I'd ask them to check the facts.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the other team contests your account? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd ask what's different and correct anything that is wrong right away. If we disagree about a judgment, I'd write both views in a neutral line and offer a call with the three of us. My aim is for leaders to have the same picture we have.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c32-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented, and all scenarios are Illustrative; use only what is true in your own work.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-01 · Rebuild an answer, answer first</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite this into an answer-first version of 60 to 90 words: &quot;So we started by looking at the data and we noticed a few things, and then we had a couple of meetings with the team, and after a lot of back and forth we decided to cut the number of fields in the sign-up form, and then we tested it, and I think it did better, something like six points.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Practice with a partner.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent two supporting facts and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-01 and Worked example 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Cutting the sign-up form from 12 fields to 7 raised completion by 6 points, from 52% to 58% (invented). Two things drove it. First, the funnel showed 38% of people stopped at the address step, so we removed the fields that could wait until after sign-up. Second, we added inline validation so that errors appeared as people typed. The test ran for three weeks on a 50% split and the lift held each week. Next I'd like to test the same idea on the payment step.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Lead with the next step if the interviewer asks about plans.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Answer-first can sound abrupt; a warm, clear first sentence solves that.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-02 · Rewrite a status update</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite as a four-line update (FW-C32-03): &quot;This week was busy! I had lots of meetings with the team and talked to a few customers. We're still working on the dashboard and I think it'll be done soon. There were some issues with the data that I'm looking into. I also reviewed the designs. Next week I'll keep working on all of this. Let me know if you need anything.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A weekly update.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Invent facts and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;<strong>At risk: dashboard launch may slip two days because of a data issue; one decision needed.</strong> <strong>Changed:</strong> Customer calls (5) showed that the weekly view is the one people want, so we will ship that first; a data issue (duplicate events for 3% of users) is being fixed by engineering. <strong>Next:</strong> fix verified Tuesday; designs approved Wednesday. <strong>Need from you:</strong> Decide by Tuesday whether to launch the weekly view without the monthly view.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If the data issue is minor, change the headline to &quot;On track&quot; and move the issue to &quot;Changed.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A candid headline invites questions; it also lets people help.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-03 · Write a decision document</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a one-page decision document (FW-C32-02) for: &quot;Ship the new onboarding on Monday with a known minor bug (affects 1% of users, cosmetic), or delay a week.&quot; Invent supporting numbers and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A real decision memo.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ten minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-02 and Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;<strong>Decision needed:</strong> Ship Monday with a cosmetic bug or delay a week; needed by Friday, from the head of product. <strong>Context:</strong> The new onboarding raised completion from 41% to 47% in a test; a cosmetic misalignment affects 1% of users (about 600 a month); a one-week delay would cost about 1,200 additional completions at the 6-point lift (invented). <strong>Options:</strong> A: ship Monday and fix in the next release (cost: 600 users a month see a misaligned button; risk: low). B: delay a week (cost: about 1,200 fewer completions; risk: none from the bug). C: ship Monday to 50% and fix within three days. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> C, which captures about half the gain immediately and exposes about 300 users a month to a cosmetic issue for three days. <strong>What would change my mind:</strong> if support reports confusion, or if the bug affects more than 3% of users. <strong>Risks and next steps:</strong> engineering fixes by Wednesday; support has a note; I review daily.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Recommend A with a support note if the bug is truly cosmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A staged release reduces exposure and adds coordination.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-04 · Map the stakeholders</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You propose raising the price of a plan by 10%. Fill in a stakeholder map (FW-C32-05) for six stakeholders: finance, sales, support, engineering, marketing and the head of product (decider), with power, interest, concern, likely position and your plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A contested change.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-05 and Worked example 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Finance:</em> high power, high interest; wants revenue and forecast accuracy; champion; give the model and the range. <em>Sales:</em> medium power, high interest; fears harder deals and discount requests; skeptic; ask what discount data shows and agree guardrails. <em>Support:</em> low power, high interest; fears angry customers; neutral; prepare a script and a volume estimate. <em>Engineering:</em> medium power, low interest; wants the change to be a configuration, not code; neutral; confirm it is simple. <em>Marketing:</em> medium power, medium interest; messaging and timing; neutral; co-write the message. <em>head of product:</em> high power, medium interest; wants a defensible decision; pre-wire with the decision document and the test plan (price test on new customers first).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add customers as a stakeholder group and plan a communication.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A thorough map costs an afternoon and avoids a late blocker.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-05 · Sequence the pre-wiring</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You need engineering, legal and a skeptical growth lead to support a new consent step. In what order do you talk to them, what do you ask each, and what do you say to the skeptic?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Influence without authority.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Legal first:</em> confirm the requirement in writing, so that the proposal is not just a preference. <em>Analyst:</em> get a range of the impact. <em>Growth lead second:</em> the skeptic should not hear it from someone else; acknowledge the loss, show the range, propose a test with a guardrail, and ask what result would persuade them. <em>Engineering third:</em> scope, risk and a rollback flag. Then design, then the decider. To the skeptic: &quot;I agree the loss matters; that's why I want to test two designs. If the lighter one costs under half a point we ship it; if not, we change the design. Counsel has confirmed doing nothing isn't an option here.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Talk to the engineer first if the effort estimate is the likeliest blocker.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The order is a judgment: start with whoever's input changes the proposal most.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-06 · Write three no scripts</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write short no scripts (FW-C32-07) for: (a) a sales director asking for a custom report in two days; (b) your manager asking for a feature in addition to a full plan; (c) a stakeholder asking you to hide a known bug from a customer.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Saying no.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;I see the goal: the customer needs numbers for their review. I can't build the report in two days; it's about a week. I can send you a spreadsheet extract today that covers most of it, and I'll build the report if two more customers ask. Does the extract work for their review?&quot; (b) &quot;We can add it, and to hold the date something leaves. Options: drop the export, or move the launch by two weeks. Which do you prefer?&quot; (c) &quot;I can't do that: hiding a known bug from a customer risks their trust and our obligations. What I can do is tell them about it with the workaround and the fix date; I'll draft the message now, and we can send it together.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> For (a), offer a time-boxed manual version with a clear end date.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A firm no on (c) may cost goodwill; the cost of hiding it is higher.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-07 · State the uncertainty</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A test on 6,000 users shows a 1.8% lift in conversion with a 95% interval of −0.2% to +3.8%. The change can be rolled back in a day. Write the uncertainty statement (FW-C32-08) and a recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Incomplete data.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C32-08 and IQ-C32-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The interval is about ±2 points wide, so the standard error is about 1.0 point, and the estimate is about 1.8 standard errors above zero; under a simple normal approximation the chance the true lift is positive is about 96%. &quot;Based on a test of 6,000 users, conversion rose 1.8% (interval −0.2% to +3.8%). I'm about 90% confident the effect is positive; I round down because we have only two weeks of data and haven't seen the mobile app separately. I expect a true effect between 0% and 3%. The change is reversible within a day, so I recommend shipping to 25% of users and checking on Friday; if conversion falls by more than 1%, we roll back. What would change my mind: a negative reading on mobile.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Extend the test a week if the cost of waiting is small and the change is not easily reversible.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A confident statement invites challenge; that is the point.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-08 · Meeting notes and follow-up</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> From these raw notes, write a follow-up message (decisions, owners, dates, open questions): &quot;Talked about launch. Priya says the bug is minor. Sam thinks we should wait. Leo said legal needs a day. We'll probably go Monday unless something comes up. Someone should tell marketing. Open: whether to turn off export.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> After a meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C32.9.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Thanks all. <strong>Decisions:</strong> Launch Monday, subject to legal sign-off by Friday noon (Leo). <strong>Owners:</strong> Leo, legal review, Friday noon. Priya, confirm the bug is cosmetic by Thursday and share the repro steps. I will tell marketing today. <strong>Open question:</strong> whether to turn off export for launch; Sam and Priya to bring a recommendation by Thursday; I decide Friday. <strong>Risk raised:</strong> Sam's concern that a bug could affect first impressions; mitigation: staged rollout to 25%. Please reply with corrections by tomorrow.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a one-line summary at the top if the thread is long.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Precision on owners and dates takes a minute and prevents the &quot;I thought you were doing it&quot; problem.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-09 · Time-zone arithmetic and handoff</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You work from India (IST, UTC+5:30) with a nine-to-six day. Compute the overlap with a London team (nine to six, GMT in winter, UTC+1 in summer) and with a California team (nine to six Pacific, UTC−8 in winter). Propose a plan for the California team.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Remote work.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C32.10.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Your day is 03:30 to 12:30 UTC. London winter (GMT): 09:00 to 18:00 UTC, overlap 09:00 to 12:30: 3.5 hours; London summer (BST, UTC+1): 08:00 to 17:00 UTC, overlap 08:00 to 12:30: 4.5 hours. California winter (UTC−8): 17:00 to 02:00 UTC, no overlap with your day; California 06:30 is 14:30 UTC, which is 20:00 in India. So a live meeting needs the Californian to start at 06:30 to 08:30 and you to work 20:00 to 22:00. Plan: write for asynchronous reading with the ask and deadline first; one live call a week, alternating inconvenience (one week your evening, the next their early morning); a daily written handoff at the end of each day; a decision log; delegates who can decide in each region.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Shift your own day one or two days a week (for example to 12:00 to 21:00) so that the overlap is shorter and less lopsided.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fairness in awkward hours matters for morale and for the quality of meetings.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c32-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C32-10 · A timed executive update with interruptions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> With a partner as a senior leader, give a five-minute update on a launch that is at risk, with at least two interruptions (one hostile, one off-topic). Use FW-C32-06 and record yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use a real or invented project.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes plus two minutes of questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C32.5 and FW-C32-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A good performance: the answer and ask in the first sentence; two or three reasons with the key number; options and a recommendation; the risk and the deadline; on interruption, a direct answer to the question, then &quot;to come back to the decision&quot;; on a hostile challenge, acknowledgment (&quot;that's fair&quot;), a factual answer and no defensiveness; an off-topic question parked politely (&quot;I'll follow up on that after this&quot;); a restatement of the decision and owner at the end; a written follow-up within a day. Typical problems found: background first, no deadline, defending instead of answering, and not restating the ask.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run the update at one minute and again at five to test whether the structure holds at both lengths.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A one-minute version forces clarity; a five-minute version rewards depth.</p>\n<span id=\"c32-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: answer first and writing.</em> Use IQ-C32-01, IQ-C32-02, EX-C32-01, EX-C32-02 and EX-C32-03. Record yourself summarizing three projects in 60 seconds with the answer in the first sentence, and rewrite three real updates.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: influence, stakeholders and conflict.</em> Use IQ-C32-03, IQ-C32-05, IQ-C32-06, IQ-C32-10, EX-C32-04, EX-C32-05 and EX-C32-06. Map the stakeholders of a real decision at work and write three no scripts for requests you actually get.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: seniors, uncertainty, remote and live.</em> Use IQ-C32-04, IQ-C32-07, IQ-C32-08, IQ-C32-09, IQ-C32-11, EX-C32-07, EX-C32-08, EX-C32-09 and EX-C32-10. Run a five-minute executive update with a partner who interrupts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;Get to the point,&quot; &quot;What do you need from me?&quot; and &quot;How sure are you?&quot; at random, and to cut you off at 90 seconds. Practice giving the answer, the ask and the confidence in one breath.</p>\n<p><strong>Test your writing.</strong> Give a document to someone outside the project and ask them to say the point and the ask after one read. If they cannot, rewrite.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I lead with the answer or the ask within fifteen seconds?</li>\n<li>Were my supports two to four, named up front and ordered?</li>\n<li>Did I include a number with a baseline and say what I don't know?</li>\n<li>Did my writing let a reader act, with the ask, owner and deadline clear?</li>\n<li>Did I show I understood the other person's incentives and goal?</li>\n<li>Did I disagree with facts and criteria, and commit after the decision?</li>\n<li>Did I say no with an alternative and a path to yes?</li>\n<li>Did I state my confidence and what would change my mind?</li>\n<li>Did I handle interruptions by answering, then returning to my point?</li>\n<li>Did I send a written follow-up with decisions, owners and dates?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the second at which you gave the answer, the number and ask you included, the interruption you handled worst, your score out of ten and the one fix for next time.</p>\n<span id=\"c32-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as evidence for your stories.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Rewrite your next three status updates in the four-line format (FW-C32-03). Ask a reader whether they know the status and what you need after reading the headline.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Write one decision document (FW-C32-02) for a real decision, however small, with options, a recommendation and what would change it. Ask two people outside the project to say the point after one read; cut a third.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Map the stakeholders of one live project (FW-C32-05): power, interest, concerns, likely position, plan. Identify the one you have not spoken to who could block you.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Pre-wire one proposal: speak one-to-one to the two most likely objectors before the meeting, record their points, and change the proposal where they are right. Note the difference in the meeting.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Keep a log of requests you decline and use the no structure (FW-C32-07) on each; note which led to a path to yes.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Use the uncertainty statement (FW-C32-08) in one real recommendation, with a confidence number, a reversal condition and a check date; follow up on the date.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> After every meeting send decisions, owners and dates within a day. If you work across time zones, write a daily handoff for two weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the rewritten updates (a time you made a message actionable), the decision document and its review (a time you drove a decision with a written proposal), and the pre-wiring log (a time you influenced without authority). Keep what you did separate from what the team did, and keep every number real.</p>\n<span id=\"c32-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c32-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Lead with the answer or the ask, name two to four supports, order them logically and close with the ask: the structure the listener experiences matters as much as the content, and it works for speaking and for writing.</li>\n<li>Write so that a reader can act: a status update with a headline, what changed, what is next and what you need; a decision document with the ask, options with costs, a recommendation and what would change it; and cut a third.</li>\n<li>Influence without authority comes from understanding incentives, finding a shared goal, bringing evidence with its limits, pre-wiring the people who will react, making a clear small ask, and giving credit; map stakeholders early, including legal, security and support.</li>\n<li>Handle disagreement by restating the other view, separating facts from values, agreeing the evidence and criteria in advance, committing after the decision and escalating openly for real harm; say no with the goal acknowledged, a reason, an alternative and a path to yes.</li>\n<li>State uncertainty plainly with a number, the reversibility, what would change your mind and a check date; deliver bad news early with impact and a plan; and make sure dissent reaches the person who decides, in writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c32-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Barbara Minto, <em>The Pyramid Principle</em> (the original statement of the approach) <code>[V: Financial Times/Prentice Hall, third edition 2009 (earlier editions from Minto International, 1987), ISBN 9780273710516]</code>.</li>\n<li>Amazon's 2017 letter to shareholders, on narratively structured six-page memos, from Amazon's own site. The source for the writing practice in C32.2 <code>[V: Amazon 2017 shareholder letter]</code>.</li>\n<li>Report of the Presidential Commission on the Space Shuttle Challenger Accident (1986), Chapter III, &quot;The Accident,&quot; and Chapter V, &quot;The Contributing Cause of the Accident,&quot; with the testimony on the pre-launch teleconference (nasa.gov/history/rogersrep/). The primary source for CS-C32-02.</li>\n<li>The analysis by Wade Robison, Roger Boisjoly, David Hoeker and Stefan Young, &quot;Representation and Misrepresentation: Tufte and the Morton Thiokol Engineers on the Challenger&quot; (<em>Science and Engineering Ethics</em>, 2002; also posted by the Online Ethics Center), for the scholarly dispute about the charts <code>[V: Robison, Boisjoly, Hoeker and Young, Science and Engineering Ethics 8(1), 59 to 81 (2002); posted by the Online Ethics Center]</code>.</li>\n<li>Edward Tufte, <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997), for his critique of the Challenger charts and principles of presenting evidence <code>[V: Graphics Press, 1997, ISBN 9780961392123, 156 pages]</code>.</li>\n<li>Roger Fisher and William Ury, <em>Getting to Yes</em> (1981), on separating interests from positions in disagreement <code>[V: Houghton Mifflin, 1981, ISBN 9780395317570; later editions add Bruce Patton as co-author]</code>.</li>\n<li>Robert Cialdini, <em>Influence</em> (1984, later editions), on reciprocity and other levers, read with care about manipulation <code>[V: Cialdini, Influence, 1984]</code>.</li>\n<li>Chip Heath and Dan Heath, <em>Made to Stick</em> (2007), on the curse of knowledge and making messages clear <code>[V: Random House, 2 January 2007, ISBN 9781400064281]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"part-x\"></span>\n";
